<section xml:id="section-permutation-groups">
  <title>Permutation Groups</title>
    <subsection xml:id="subsection-permutation-definition">
    <title>Permutation Groups - Definition and Examples</title>
    <p>
      The next type of group to introduce are permutation groups.
      These are, in a sense, something that we've already considered
      before. A permutaiton is just a bijection map from a set to
      itself: it is a way of reordering the set. As such, these are
      function groups. But presenting these groups as permutations
      groups has many advantages, as well as a long history in group
      theory. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let<m>A</m> be a set. The <term>permutation group on
          <m>A</m></term> of the <term>symmetric group on
          <m>A</m></term> is the set of bijective functions from
          <m>A</m> to itself. Its notation is <m>Sym(A)</m>.  If the
          set is <m>A = \{1,2,3 \ldots, n \}</m>, then the permutation
          group is often called the <term>symmetric group on <m>n</m>
          letter</term> and given the notation <m>S_n</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      The finite case, <m>S_n</m>, will be where we spend our time int
      this course. I want to setup some standard terms and notations
      to help us understand this group. 
    </p>
    <p>
      I'll start with permutation notation: how do I write a
      permutation? I can do something like I did with vertices and
      dihedral groups (note! dihedral groups are just permutations of
      vertices <m>\ldots</m>), where I draw arrows showing where each
      set element goes. That works, but there are some more succint
      standard notations. The first is Cauchy's notation. Here is a
      permutation in <m>S_7</m>. (Lower case greek letters are pretty
      common for permutations.) 
      <me>
        \sigma = \begin{pmatrix} 
          1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 5 \amp 6 \amp 7 \\
          2 \amp 7 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 6 \amp 5 \amp 3 
        \end{pmatrix} 
      </me>
      This is the permutation that sends 1 to 2, 2 to 7, 3 to 1, 4 to
      itself, 5 to 6, 6 to 5 and 7 to 3. (Feel free to check that this
      is, indeed, bijective.) To speak generally, in Cauchy's
      notation, the top line is always the usual ordered list and the
      bottom line is the destination of each element under the
      permutation. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is pretty good notation, but there is something even more
      concise and even more common. Look at the example again. It has
      one fixed point, 4. It has one loop of length four: 1 goes to 2,
      then 2 to 7, then 7 to 3, then 3 to 1. And then it has another
      loop of length 2: 5 and 6 switch with each other. Here is the
      cycle notation for the permutation.
      <me>
        (1273)(4)(56)
      </me>
      Cycle notation identifies the loops in the permutation and write
      those between brackets. A fixed point is its own bracket, like
      <m>(4)</m> (though often they are just ommited entirely). A
      switch is something with two numbers, like <m>(56)</m>. Longer
      cycles loop through more numbers. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Cycle notation will be the way that we analyze (finite)
      permutation groups. It takes some getting used to, but it is
      efficient and clear. Let me show you some examples of how to use
      cycle notation. In all the examples, I'll keep working in
      <m>S_7</m>. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        The identity can be write in cycle notation as follows. 
        <me>
          \Id = (1)(2)(3)(4)(5)(6)(7)
        </me>
        Again, usually fixed points are not written at all, and the
        <m>\Id</m> is still the identity. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The permutation that fixed 1, 3 and 7; switches 4 and 6; and
        switched 2 and 5 has this cycle representation.
        <me>
          \sigma = (25)(46) 
        </me>
        I am not ommiting the fixed points: since they don't show up in
        the cycle representation, 1, 3 and 7 all don't change, which is
        understood by their absence here. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, the permutation that increases each number by one, and
        loops around for 7, is given by this cycle. 
        <me>
          \sigma = (1234567)
        </me>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        I can also use cycles to compose permutations. This takes a bit
        of thinking, but let me demonstrate. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Say I want to calculate <m>\sigma \tau</m>, remembing that the
        right most permutation happens first. Let's say that <m>\sigma =
        (15)(263)</m> and <m>\tau = (234)(57)</m>. Then I want to
        calculate this product.
        <me>
          \sigma \tau = (15)(263)(234)(57)
        </me>
        How do I do this? We'll, I'll work step by step. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            First, where does 1 go under this permutation? I work from
            right to left, looking for the first place that 1 appears.
            It only appears in the last step, where it is sent to 5.
            Therefore, 1 goes to 5. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Now where does 2 go? Working from the right, 2 is in the
            second cycle, where it is snet to 3. Then I keep moving
            left, now looking for 3. 3 is in the next cycle where it is
            sent to 2. Then, still moving left, 2 does not appear again.
            I conclude that 2 goes to 2 in this composition.
          </li>
          <li>
            Now where does 3 go? From the right, the second cycle
            sents 3 to 4, and then 4 does not show up again moving left.
            Therefore, 3 goes to 4. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Now where does 4 go? From the right, the second cycle
            sends 4 to 2 and then the next cycle sents 2 to 6. 6
            doesn't appear again moving left, so 4 goes to 6. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Now where does 5 go? From the right, the first cycle
            sends 5 to 7 and 7 doesn't appear again in any other cycle,
            so 5 goes to 7. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Now where does 6 go? From the right, I get to the third
            cycle where 6 is sent to 3. Then 3 doesn't appear again, so
            6 goes to three. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Finally, where does 7 go? The first cycle sents 7 to 5, then
            the last cycle sends 5 to 1. Therefore, 7 goes to 1. 
          </li>
        </ul>
        Now I put this all together. 1 goes to 5, 5 to 7, and 7 to 1. So
        I have a cycle <m>(157)</m>. 2 is fixed, so it doesn't show up.
        3 goes to 4, 4 to 6, and 6 to 3, so I have a cycle <m>(346)</m>.
        That's everything. here is the result. 
        <me>
          \sigma \tau = (15)(263)(234)(57) = (157)(346) 
        </me>
      </p>
      <p> 
        I could also calculate the reverse composition, <m>\tau
        \sigma</m>.
        <me>
          \tau \sigma = (234)(57)(15)(263) 
        </me>
        Let me run through the piece, with a little less commentary this
        time. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            1 goes to 5, then 5 to 7. So 1 is sent to 7.
          </li>
          <li>
            2 goes to 6 with no more movements. So 2 is sent to 6. 
          </li>
          <li>
            3 goes to 2 and then 2 to 3. So 3 is fixed. 
          </li>
          <li>
            4 goes to 2 with no more movements. So 4 is sent to 2. 
          </li>
          <li>
            5 goes to 1 with no move movements. So 5 is sent to 1. 
          </li>
          <li>
            6 goes to 3, then to 4. So 6 is sent to 4. 
          </li>
          <li>
            7 goes to 5 with no move movements. So 7 is sent to 5. 
          </li>
        </ul>
        The I summarize these movements as cycles. 1 goes to 7, 7 to 5
        and then 5 to 1, so I have a cycle (175). 2 goes to 6, 6 to 4,
        and then 4 to 2, so I have a cycle (264). 3 is fixed. Putting
        these cycles together gives me the composition. 
        <me>
          \tau \sigma = (234)(57)(15)(263) = (175)(264) 
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        I see, in this process, that <m>\tau \sigma \neq \sigma
        \tau</m>. As with matrix groups, permutations groups are a good
        source of interseting non-abelian group structures. In general,
        I expect that most permutations do not commute with each other.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Having done some examples, let me move on to stating (and perhaps
      proving) some of the important facts about cycles. The previous
      example shows that permutations do not, in general, commute. Is
      there an issue, then, in how I write cycle decomposition? I wrote
      the decomposition of the last calculation as <m>(175)(264)</m>,
      but should it have been written <m>(264)(175)</m>? Thankfully, the
      following proposition shows that I don't have to worry. 
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="proposition-disjoint-cycles">>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two cycles representing permutations are <term>disjoint</term>
          if they have no numbers in common. With this definition, it is
          true that disjoint cycles commute. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I can make a pretty straightforward argument here. Let
          <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be disjouint cyclesn <m>S_n</m>. Then
          written <m>ab</m> means that <m>b</m> happens first. <m>b</m>
          will act on the numbers present in its notation. Then <m>a</m>
          happens second, and <m>a</m> will again act on the numbers
          present in its notation. Since the cycles are disjoint,
          nothing in the first movement affects the second: they are
          moving entirely different subsets of <m>\{1,2, \ldots n
          \}</m>. If they were reversed, likewise nothing in the second
          cycle affect anything in the first. They are independent
          permutations of different subsets. Therefore, they can happen
          in any order with the same total effect. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-cycle-decomposition">
    <title>Cycle Decomposition</title>
    <p>
      One of the main overarching problems in groups theory (and in
      abstract mathematics in general) is one of
      <emph>representation</emph>. What is the best way to represent a
      mathematical definition, such as a permutation? Both Cauchy
      notation and cycle decomposition gives representations for
      permutations. We want these representations to be unique, since
      multiple ways to write the same object leads to confusion.
      (Think again of the challenges of multiple ways to write each
      rational number, which is solved by unique representation of
      reduced fractions.) Are cycle decompositions unique? They can
      be, but with some subtleties. First, this is fairly easy to see
      with disjoint cycles. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p> 
          Let <m>\sigma \in S_n</m>. Then, up to reordering of the
          both the multiplication of cycles and shifting the numbers
          in the cycles themselves, <m>\sigma</m> can be uniquely
          written as a product of disjoint cycles. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          First, I know that I can produce a disjoint cycle
          representation. The algorithm that produces is is as
          follows. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              Start with 1. Write the cycle <m>(1 \ldots )</m> determined
              by the image of 1, then the image of that, and so on
              until 1 is reached again.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then choose the smallest number <m>k</m> which was not
              in the first cycle. Repeat the process making the cycle
              <m>(k \ldots )</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              Repeat this process until there are no numbers left.
              Since the original list is finite, this can always be
              completed
                Repeat this process until there are no numbers left.
                Since the original list is finite, this can always be
                completed
            </li>
          </ul>
          Therefore, I can always produce a representation as disjoint
          cycles. 
        </p>
        <p>
          I know from <xref ref="proposition-disjoint-cycles"/> that
          disjoint cycles commute. Therefore, any order of the product
          of disjoint cycles is the same permutation. This covers
          reordering of the product of cycles.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now for the reodering of the cycles themselves. A cycle is a
          loop, since the end of the cycle is sent back to the start.
          As a loop, the starting point is arbitrary. (For example,
          these cycles all do exactly the same thing: <m>(1375),
          (3751), (7513), (3751)</m>). Therefore, a shift of the
          numbers is a cycle doesn't change the cycle, and the
          presentation by cycles is unique only up to this shifting.
          (You could also impose a restriction here to make this a
          unique notation: say, a cycle must always start with the
          smallest number in its list.) 
        </p>
        <p>
          So I have a cycle decomposition and I can reorder it in both
          ways. Is it actually unique, though? Can there be another
          way to write the same permutation as difference disjoint
          cycles? There cannot, and the algorithm presented earliert is
          actually an argument for this. In that algorithm, I started
          with the number 1. Where 1 goes is determined by the
          function, so the next element of its cycle is fixed.
          Likewise for the next, and all the way down the cycle. The
          only variance is the starting element of the cycle, as
          discussed in the previous paragraph. But the cycles
          themselves are entirely determined by the permutation and,
          therefore, are unique. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
</section>
